[
  {
    "runtag": "cfda-adarewriter-chiq-llm4cs-splade",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can breastfeeding be harmful for infants in any way?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Breastfeeding can be harmful to infants in certain situations. For instance, if a mother has specific medical conditions, takes certain medications, or uses harmful drugs, these can be passed through breast milk and may pose risks to the baby. Additionally, infections that the mother might have can also be transmitted through breastfeeding. It's essential for mothers to consult with healthcare providers if they have concerns about their health conditions or medications to ensure breastfeeding is safe for their infants. However, in general, breastfeeding is associated with numerous health benefits for both mothers and infants.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0000-85-06393:13": 1.893357276916504,
            "clueweb22-en0002-28-05112:9": 1.5400663614273071,
            "clueweb22-en0034-72-03542:2": 1.0460909605026245,
            "clueweb22-en0002-28-05112:8": 0.8061997890472412,
            "clueweb22-en0035-03-10623:4": 0.6217421889305115,
            "clueweb22-en0000-85-06393:12": 0.594781219959259,
            "clueweb22-en0026-86-14685:10": 0.569401204586029,
            "clueweb22-en0009-37-07271:10": 0.5569230318069458,
            "clueweb22-en0013-76-15640:15": 0.5506855249404907,
            "clueweb22-en0043-98-01457:5": 0.45207953453063965,
            "clueweb22-en0024-47-00126:6": 0.20218691229820251,
            "clueweb22-en0014-34-02584:1": 0.17499706149101257,
            "clueweb22-en0025-83-18972:2": 0.11844274401664734,
            "clueweb22-en0025-83-18972:1": 0.09072813391685486,
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-17881:1": 0.06869141757488251,
            "clueweb22-en0018-64-00250:1": 0.04186554253101349
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks! What about diet\u2014do I need to change it during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, you will need to change your diet during pregnancy to ensure both your health and the healthy development of your baby. Focus on a balanced diet that includes plenty of fruits and vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins, and calcium-rich foods. It's important to increase your intake of protein by about 25 grams per day, and include foods such as beans, lentils, tofu, nuts, and dairy products. Aim for at least 1,000-1,300 mg of calcium daily, which can be obtained from dairy products or non-dairy sources like leafy greens, almonds, and fortified alternatives. Additionally, ensure you are consuming enough iron, aiming for about 27 mg per day, which can be found in lean meats, legumes, and fortified cereals. Staying hydrated and maintaining good food hygiene are also essential. If you have specific dietary concerns or restrictions, consider consulting with a healthcare provider for personalized advice.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-37-15773:1": -1.4103552103042603
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "And after the baby is born, when breastfeeding, does the diet still need to focus on things like calcium and protein?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, after giving birth and while breastfeeding, it is essential to maintain a diet that focuses on calcium and protein, among other nutrients. Calcium is crucial for both your health and the development of your baby, as breastfeeding can deplete your calcium reserves. Aim for about 1,000 mg of calcium daily, which can be obtained from dairy products, leafy greens, and fortified foods. Additionally, you should ensure adequate protein intake, as it supports recovery from childbirth and helps in producing milk. Incorporate a variety of foods, including lean meats, beans, nuts, and dairy, to meet your nutritional needs. If you're concerned about getting enough calcium, consider discussing dietary adjustments or supplements with your healthcare provider.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-67-00902:13": 0.6274515390396118,
            "clueweb22-en0016-50-05532:10": 0.547544538974762,
            "clueweb22-en0022-19-14413:17": 0.48316800594329834,
            "clueweb22-en0019-84-02374:2": 0.4665739834308624,
            "clueweb22-en0014-81-15963:5": 0.4445340037345886,
            "clueweb22-en0015-63-11671:1": 0.31261155009269714,
            "clueweb22-en0007-63-19153:11": 0.3104950189590454,
            "clueweb22-en0027-60-18300:3": 0.19382306933403015,
            "clueweb22-en0016-79-13986:6": 0.18146073818206787,
            "clueweb22-en0030-21-06999:1": 0.0919068232178688
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. Are certain foods better for increasing milk supply while breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Yes, certain foods can help increase milk supply while breastfeeding. Foods that are often recommended include oats, brewer's yeast, and flaxseed, which are considered galactagogues\u2014substances believed to enhance breast milk production. Additionally, incorporating a variety of protein-rich foods, dark leafy greens, and legumes can support overall nutrition and milk supply. It's also important to stay well-hydrated and consume enough calories, typically an additional 400-500 calories per day, to meet the demands of breastfeeding. If you have specific dietary concerns, consulting with a healthcare provider or a lactation consultant can provide personalized guidance.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0000-09-08408:2": 1.5776840448379517,
            "clueweb22-en0002-43-08931:2": 0.809235692024231,
            "clueweb22-en0002-43-08931:1": 0.5344905853271484,
            "clueweb22-en0025-48-13143:7": 0.036010898649692535
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's helpful. Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, it is possible for someone to experience a decrease in milk supply while breastfeeding, which might make them feel like they are \"running out\" of milk. This can happen due to various factors such as stress, dehydration, insufficient caloric intake, or hormonal changes. It's important to note that many mothers may perceive low supply when they are actually producing enough milk. If you notice a drop in your supply, consider strategies like ensuring adequate hydration, eating enough calories, reducing stress, and consulting with a lactation consultant if needed. Most breastfeeding mothers find that their supply can return to normal with the right support and practices.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-40-15481:7": 1.2557047605514526,
            "clueweb22-en0041-68-17369:2": 1.1558425426483154,
            "clueweb22-en0027-40-15481:6": 0.3268943428993225,
