  {
    "runtag": "cfda-chiq-llm4cs-splade-rrf",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Is it true that breastfeeding can sometimes be harmful for babies?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, breastfeeding can sometimes be harmful for babies, particularly in certain situations. For instance, if a mother has specific medical conditions, takes certain medications, or uses harmful drugs, these can be passed to the baby through breast milk and may be harmful. Additionally, while breast milk is generally very nutritious and beneficial for infants, studies have found that it can contain trace amounts of harmful substances due to environmental toxins, which could accumulate in some mothers over time. Therefore, it's important for mothers to consult healthcare providers if they have concerns about breastfeeding safety.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0024-98-17881:1": 1.0774364471435547,
            "clueweb22-en0000-85-06393:13": 1.0591542720794678
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks. About my original question, do I actually need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, you will need to change your diet during pregnancy to ensure both you and your baby receive adequate nutrition. It's important to focus on a balanced diet that includes a variety of foods rich in essential nutrients. Key nutrients to increase include calcium, iron, and folic acid. For instance, calcium is crucial for building strong bones and teeth, and you should aim for at least 1,000 mg daily if you're over 18. You may also need to consume more protein and increase your caloric intake as your pregnancy progresses. Consulting with your healthcare provider can help you tailor your diet to meet these needs effectively.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0015-82-12229:1": 1.364088535308838,
            "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:18": 1.2541768550872803,
            "clueweb22-en0000-65-01462:3": 1.208885908126831,
            "clueweb22-en0008-88-00414:6": 1.1193263530731201,
            "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:1": 1.0360630750656128,
            "clueweb22-en0030-97-15349:3": 0.9652287364006042,
            "clueweb22-en0002-95-17855:1": 0.9587372541427612,
            "clueweb22-en0037-54-02698:7": 0.9449682235717773,
            "clueweb22-en0001-10-19534:7": 0.9251552820205688,
            "clueweb22-en0035-18-17078:5": 0.8858333826065063,
            "clueweb22-en0023-54-04375:6": 0.8833785057067871,
            "clueweb22-en0031-73-15036:4": 0.8187173008918762,
            "clueweb22-en0000-09-19416:0": 0.7802861928939819,
            "clueweb22-en0038-84-03878:1": 0.7760598063468933,
            "clueweb22-en0031-73-15036:6": 0.7759843468666077,
            "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:5": 0.7622316479682922,
            "clueweb22-en0000-65-01462:4": 0.757046639919281,
            "clueweb22-en0005-13-02598:2": 0.7491233944892883,
            "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:2": 0.676283597946167,
            "clueweb22-en0014-61-10226:3": 0.6659281253814697
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Ah, so if I\u2019m breastfeeding, should I be eating more protein and calcium, too? Are there any foods I should avoid because of the baby?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, while breastfeeding, it is important to increase your intake of protein and calcium to support both your health and your baby's development. Aim for a variety of protein sources such as lean meats, fish, eggs, dairy products, legumes, and nuts. For calcium, focus on low-fat dairy products, fortified plant-based milks, leafy greens, and other calcium-rich foods.\n\nAs for foods to avoid, limit your intake of caffeine to no more than 200mg per day (about four cups of tea or two cups of coffee) to prevent potential restlessness in your baby. Alcohol should also be consumed cautiously; aim for no more than two units per week and wait a couple of hours after drinking before breastfeeding. Additionally, be mindful of any foods that might cause allergic reactions or discomfort to your baby, such as dairy if your baby has shown sensitivity to it.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-84-02374:2": 0.6757670640945435,
            "clueweb22-en0014-81-15963:5": 0.6488510966300964,
            "clueweb22-en0040-13-16808:3": 0.3920280337333679
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it;",
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein;"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. I\u2019m kind of obsessed with getting enough calcium, so thanks for explaining that. But can someone ever, like, just run out of breast milk all of a sudden? Or does it usually decrease gradually?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, it is possible for a mother to experience a sudden drop in milk supply, but this is not the norm for most women. Typically, milk supply decreases gradually as the body adjusts to the baby's needs, especially around 6-12 weeks postpartum. However, factors such as stress, illness, or changes in routine can lead to a rapid decrease in milk production. If you notice a sudden drop, it\u2019s important to assess any changes in your lifestyle or health that might be affecting your supply. There are ways to help boost your milk supply if needed, such as increasing nursing or pumping sessions.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0027-03-18679:2": 0.516753613948822,
            "clueweb22-en0027-03-18679:1": 0.27818283438682556
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you for your thoughtful questions and for sharing your experiences! It\u2019s great that you\u2019re so proactive and caring about your health and your baby\u2019s well-being. If you ever have more questions or need guidance, don\u2019t hesitate to reach out to your healthcare provider. Wishing you all the best on your parenting journey\u2014take care and farewell!",
