  {
    "runtag": "genaius-full-rewrite",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can breastfeeding ever be harmful to babies?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Breastfeeding is generally beneficial for babies, but there are situations where it could potentially be harmful. For example, substances like alcohol, certain medications, or drugs consumed by the mother can pass into breast milk and affect the baby. Alcohol, for instance, can disrupt a baby\u2019s sleep and feeding patterns if consumed in significant amounts by the mother. Similarly, medications like benzodiazepines or marijuana can lead to sedation or other adverse effects in infants. It\u2019s also important to note that maternal health conditions, such as untreated depression, can indirectly impact the baby through reduced responsiveness or interaction. However, breastfeeding itself often provides protective benefits, even in such cases. If you have concerns about specific substances or conditions, consulting a healthcare provider is crucial to ensure the safety and well-being of both mother and baby.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0034-22-10973:0": 17.067899703979492,
            "clueweb22-en0010-16-13896:0": 16.609800338745117,
            "clueweb22-en0024-77-08049:3": 16.349199295043945
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s interesting. What about changing my diet\u2014do I need to eat differently if I\u2019m pregnant?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, during pregnancy, it\u2019s important to adjust your diet to meet the increased nutritional needs of both you and your baby. Pregnant women require more energy and nutrients, including protein, iron, calcium, and folic acid, to support the baby\u2019s development. For example, consuming more fruits, vegetables, whole grains, lean proteins, and dairy products is recommended. Foods rich in iron, such as red meat, beans, and leafy greens, are particularly important to prevent anemia, and pairing them with vitamin C-rich foods can enhance iron absorption. Additionally, folic acid is crucial for preventing neural tube defects, so incorporating fortified cereals or supplements is often advised. \n\nIt\u2019s also essential to avoid certain foods, like raw or undercooked meats, unpasteurized dairy, and high-mercury fish, to reduce the risk of foodborne illnesses and other complications. Limiting caffeine and avoiding alcohol are also recommended. Staying hydrated by drinking plenty of water is equally important. Consulting with a healthcare provider or a nutritionist can help tailor dietary recommendations to your specific needs.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-54-04375:2": 28.487199783325195,
            "clueweb22-en0023-54-04375:5": 26.477699279785156,
            "clueweb22-en0037-95-16785:0": 25.474199295043945
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "How about after pregnancy\u2014does my diet need to change again when breastfeeding? Should I be more careful about certain foods if I want to keep my calcium levels up?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, your diet should adapt during breastfeeding to meet your increased nutritional needs and support your baby\u2019s growth. Calcium is particularly important, as breastfeeding can deplete your body\u2019s calcium stores. Aim for 1,000 mg of calcium daily, which can be achieved through dairy products like milk, cheese, and yogurt, or fortified alternatives such as almond or soy milk. If you find it difficult to meet this requirement through food alone, a calcium supplement may be helpful.\n\nAdditionally, focus on nutrient-dense, protein-rich foods like lean meats, fish, eggs, legumes, and nuts to support your energy needs and overall health. Since you prefer low-carb options, incorporating leafy greens, broccoli, and almonds can provide calcium while aligning with your dietary preferences.\n\nBe mindful of foods that could affect your baby, such as excessive caffeine or alcohol, and ensure you stay hydrated. Consulting a healthcare provider or dietitian can help tailor your diet to address your calcium concerns and overall nutritional needs during breastfeeding.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0023-59-13814:3": 35.04010009765625
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein.",
            "I am interested in low-carb food.",
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for the tips! Can someone actually run out of milk or not be able to produce enough when they\u2019re breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, it is possible for some women to experience low milk supply while breastfeeding, though it is relatively rare for a mother to produce less milk than her baby needs. Factors such as poor attachment, infrequent feeding, or medical conditions like insufficient glandular tissue (IGT) can contribute to low milk production. Additionally, issues like postpartum hemorrhage, retained placenta, or certain medications may also affect supply. However, many women who worry about low supply are actually producing enough milk, as perceived low supply is a common concern.\n\nIf low supply is a genuine issue, there are strategies to increase milk production, such as breastfeeding more frequently, ensuring proper latch, and using techniques like breast compression. Consulting a lactation consultant can be invaluable in identifying and addressing the root cause. In rare cases where milk production remains insufficient, options like supplementing with formula or using a breastfeeding supplementer can help ensure the baby\u2019s nutritional needs are met while maintaining the breastfeeding relationship.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-76-04586:3": 38.07270050048828,
            "clueweb22-en0021-55-01275:0": 36.205501556396484,
            "clueweb22-en0037-76-16950:0": 32.96730041503906
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I am 25 years old."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you so much for your thoughtful questions and insights! I appreciate our conversation. If you have any more questions in the future, feel free to ask. Wishing you and your family all the best\u2014farewell!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
