  {
    "runtag": "grilllab-agentic-gpt4.1-larf-v2",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Is breastfeeding ever harmful for an infant?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "While breastfeeding offers numerous health benefits for both mother and infant, there are situations where it may be harmful. Breastfeeding can be unsafe if the mother uses harmful drugs (such as cocaine or marijuana), as these substances can pass into breast milk and potentially harm the infant's development or health [DOC-1][DOC-3]. Additionally, certain medical conditions or medications can make breastfeeding unsafe, as some infections or drugs can be transmitted through breast milk and negatively affect the baby [DOC-2]. It is important for mothers to consult healthcare providers if they have concerns about their health, medications, or substance use while breastfeeding to ensure the safety of their infant. What specific medical conditions make breastfeeding unsafe?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0036-68-00977:9": 0.032018442622950824,
            "clueweb22-en0000-85-06393:13": 0.01639344262295082,
            "clueweb22-en0008-39-05957:4": 0.01639344262295082,
            "clueweb22-en0020-99-01004:6": 0.01639344262295082
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein.",
            "I am a woman."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s a better answer, thanks! Could you list which medical conditions would make breastfeeding risky for the child?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Medical conditions that make breastfeeding unsafe for the child include: 1) Maternal HIV infection, as the virus can be transmitted through breast milk; 2) Use of illicit drugs such as heroin, methamphetamine, cocaine, or heavy alcohol use, which can transfer harmful substances to the infant; 3) Active infections like Human T-Lymphotrophic Virus (HTLV) types I and II, Marburg virus, Ebola, untreated tuberculosis, and herpes simplex or zoster with breast lesions; 4) Certain medications, such as lithium, which may require close monitoring or may be contraindicated depending on the infant's health; and 5) For infants, the genetic disorder galactosemia is a strict contraindication to breastfeeding, as they cannot metabolize galactose in breast milk. Mothers with hepatitis C should avoid breastfeeding if they have cracked or bleeding nipples, due to increased risk of transmission. Always consult a healthcare provider for guidance on specific conditions and medications [DOC-1][DOC-3][DOC-4]. What medications are considered safe or unsafe during breastfeeding?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-17-17090:10": 0.04738666351569577,
            "clueweb22-en0032-17-17090:9": 0.046649531024531024,
            "clueweb22-en0010-27-18394:0": 0.041508373136602716,
            "clueweb22-en0042-76-17292:13": 0.0405949341102169
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein.",
            "I am interested in low-carb food."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s helpful! Can you be more specific about which common medications are safe and which are unsafe for nursing mothers? Also, are any supplements or vitamins problematic for the baby through breast milk?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Many common medications are considered safe for nursing mothers, including acetaminophen (paracetamol), nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), and some disease-modifying anti-rheumatic drugs and monoclonal antibodies, as these are unlikely to transfer into breast milk in significant amounts [DOC-4]. However, certain medications\u2014such as some psychiatric drugs, chemotherapy agents, and lithium\u2014may be unsafe and should only be used under medical supervision. Regarding supplements, most minerals like iron, calcium, and copper are generally safe and do not significantly affect breast milk levels [DOC-5]. However, fat-soluble vitamins (A and E) can concentrate in breast milk, and excessive intake may be harmful to the baby [DOC-2][DOC-3]. Vitamin D supplementation is usually safe within recommended doses, but very high doses can cause toxicity in infants, so it's important not to exceed guidelines [DOC-1][DOC-2]. Always consult a healthcare provider before starting any new medication or supplement while breastfeeding. Would you like a list of specific medications to avoid while breastfeeding?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0003-06-14948:0": 0.04057453201288817,
            "clueweb22-en0021-94-09366:1": 0.031746031746031744,
            "clueweb22-en0021-94-09366:0": 0.030621785881252923,
            "clueweb22-en0023-91-09701:10": 0.029957522915269395,
            "clueweb22-en0021-94-09366:2": 0.02900988017658188
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it.",
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "No, that\u2019s okay. I\u2019m more curious about dietary changes in pregnancy. Is it true you need extra nutrients, like more calcium, when pregnant?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Pregnancy increases your need for several nutrients, especially calcium, protein, folate, iron, zinc, iodine, and omega-3 fats. For calcium, women aged 19-50 are recommended to get at least 1,000 mg per day, but during pregnancy, the need rises to 1,200\u20131,400 mg daily to support the baby's bone development and maintain the mother's bone health. While your body becomes more efficient at absorbing calcium during pregnancy, many women still need to increase their intake through diet (dairy, leafy greens, fortified foods) or supplements, as prenatal vitamins often do not provide enough calcium on their own. Other key nutrients to focus on include protein, iron, folate, and omega-3s, which are vital for fetal growth and brain development. A balanced, nutrient-dense diet is essential during pregnancy to meet these increased requirements and support a healthy pregnancy outcome [DOC-1][DOC-2][DOC-6]. What are the signs of nutrient deficiencies during pregnancy?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0019-17-15515:0": 0.03724381092444897,
            "clueweb22-en0001-59-03349:1": 0.032018442622950824,
            "clueweb22-en0029-97-11935:2": 0.03177805800756621,
            "clueweb22-en0017-78-11965:6": 0.0315136476426799
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": [
            "I think I don't get enough calcium, and I am worried about it.",
            "I prefer foods with more nutrients and protein.",
            "I am interested in low-carb food."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s exactly what I was wondering\u2014I think I\u2019m not getting enough calcium! What happens if you don\u2019t get enough calcium during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Calcium deficiency during pregnancy can have serious consequences for both mother and baby. For mothers, low calcium intake is associated with an increased risk of preeclampsia\u2014a dangerous condition characterized by high blood pressure and organ dysfunction\u2014which can be life-threatening if untreated [DOC-2][DOC-3]. Inadequate calcium can also lead to bone loss (osteopenia or osteoporosis) and increase the risk of fractures, as your body will draw calcium from your bones to support the baby's needs [DOC-3]. For the baby, insufficient calcium can impair the development of bones and muscles, and may increase the risk of osteoporosis later in life [DOC-1][DOC-4]. Symptoms of calcium deficiency in pregnancy include muscle aches, cramps, fatigue, brittle nails, and dental problems [DOC-4]. To prevent these issues, it's recommended to consume 1,000\u20131,300 mg of calcium daily during pregnancy, through diet or supplements if needed. What are the best dietary sources of calcium for pregnancy?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0043-42-12916:2": 0.058932015646405655,
            "clueweb22-en0043-28-13019:1": 0.04858377154202925,
