          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s fantastic, thanks! I actually eat a lot of almonds and tofu, and sometimes I have sardines for lunch with my white rice. I guess the rice isn\u2019t very low-carb though\u2026 I also like jam and white bread, but I know those aren\u2019t really nutrient-dense. Do you think having juice or cake as an occasional snack has any impact on my calcium or nutrient intake during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Enjoying cake, juice, or other sweet snacks occasionally is generally not harmful during pregnancy, especially if your overall diet is balanced and nutrient-rich. However, regularly eating high-sugar foods like cake, jam, white bread, and juice can displace more nutrient-dense foods, potentially reducing your intake of essential nutrients such as calcium, iron, and protein [clueweb22-en0023-90-18032:13]. Excess sugar intake during pregnancy is also linked to higher risks of gestational diabetes, larger birth weight, and increased risk of allergies or asthma in the child [clueweb22-en0017-44-12789:9]. While these snacks don't directly lower calcium absorption, they don't contribute much to your calcium needs and can make it harder to meet your daily requirements if they replace healthier options. Moderation is key\u2014enjoy your favorite treats, but try to prioritize calcium-rich, nutrient-dense foods most of the time [clueweb22-en0015-88-09308:3].",
