          "text": "Milk supply is primarily driven by how often and effectively your baby nurses or milk is expressed, as well as staying well-hydrated. However, consistently eating a poor, nutrient-deficient diet can impact the quality of your breast milk\u2014levels of certain vitamins (like B vitamins, vitamin A, and iodine) in milk can decrease if your intake is low, potentially affecting your baby's nutrition [clueweb22-en0010-89-07259:6]. Severe or prolonged undernutrition may also reduce your energy levels and, in rare cases, can lead to decreased milk production, especially if calorie or protein intake is very low [clueweb22-en0003-91-09593:1]. For most women, occasional lapses in diet won't cause milk to 'run out,' but a consistently unbalanced diet can have negative effects for both mother and baby. Prioritizing nutrient-dense foods supports both milk quality and your own health during breastfeeding. Are there specific signs that my milk supply is low?",
