          "text": "Most women are capable of producing enough milk for their babies, and true inability to produce enough milk is uncommon. Low milk supply can be caused by factors such as delayed breastfeeding after delivery, poor latch, infrequent feeding, certain medical conditions (like hypothyroidism or polycystic ovarian syndrome), use of some medications, or previous breast surgery. Sometimes, mothers may perceive they have low supply due to normal baby behaviors like frequent feeding or cluster feeding, but these are not necessarily signs of insufficient milk. If a baby is gaining weight and having enough wet diapers, milk supply is usually adequate. Support and proper breastfeeding techniques can help address most supply concerns, but in rare cases, underlying medical issues may require specialist help [clueweb22-en0021-55-01275][clueweb22-en0037-76-04586][clueweb22-en0026-25-06744]. What are the best ways to increase breast milk supply?",
