      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really important to keep in mind! Can stress affect breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 4,
          "text": "Stress can absolutely affect breastfeeding, both physically and emotionally\u2014but there are also protective effects to be aware of, especially given your focus on nutrient-rich, high-protein, low-carb foods and calcium intake.\n\n**How Stress Impacts Breastfeeding:**\n- **Milk Supply & Letdown:** High stress can inhibit the release of oxytocin, the hormone responsible for milk letdown, making it harder for milk to flow. Chronic stress or depression may also reduce overall milk supply.\n- **Breastfeeding Difficulties:** Stress and fatigue can make breastfeeding more challenging, which in turn can increase the risk of postpartum depression. Painful or difficult breastfeeding experiences are linked to higher rates of depression and early weaning.\n- **Physical Effects:** Stress can contribute to issues like mastitis (breast infection), especially if it leads to missed feeds or poor milk drainage.\n\n**Protective Effects of Breastfeeding Against Stress:**\n- **Hormonal Benefits:** Breastfeeding releases oxytocin and prolactin, which have natural anti-stress and antidepressant effects, helping lower anxiety and promote positive mood.\n- **",
