          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful! I\u2019m curious\u2014can someone actually run out of breast milk, or does the body just keep making more if the baby keeps eating?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "The body will continue to produce milk as long as the baby is feeding regularly. The contact between the baby's mouth and the mother's breast stimulates a physiological response that prompts the breasts to create milk. This means that the mother's body will make enough milk to meet the demand of her baby, and the milk production will adjust accordingly. As long as the baby is nursing, the mother's body will keep producing milk to satisfy the baby's needs. However, if the baby stops feeding on a regular schedule, milk secretion will be reduced. But if the mother continues to express milk or feed another baby, her body will continue to produce milk. In fact, some women can produce milk for several years, even after their child has stopped nursing, as long as they continue to stimulate their breasts through regular feeding or expression. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue Reading Related Answer. Continue",
