      {
        "turn": 6,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I see! If I don\u2019t get enough calcium, could that harm my breast milk or my baby?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "Generally, your body prioritizes your baby's calcium needs. Even if your calcium intake is low, your body will draw calcium from your bones to ensure your breast milk contains sufficient levels for your baby. Therefore, your baby's calcium intake from breast milk is usually not directly harmed. However, this process can lead to a loss of bone density for you, the mother. Calcium supplements would primarily benefit your bone health rather than directly impacting breast milk calcium content, unless your intake is extremely low (below 300 mg/day).",
