      {
        "turn": 7,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that helps. Can someone actually run out of breast milk completely, or does it always come back if you try more?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes, breast milk supply can eventually dry up completely if milk removal stops, typically within 7 to 10 days, though some may notice drops for weeks or months. However, your body continuously produces milk, and the supply is demand-driven. If your milk supply drops or even ceases, it's often possible to re-establish or increase it through consistent milk removal, either by frequent breastfeeding or pumping. This process is known as relactation.",
