      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense. What should mothers eat to make sure breast milk has enough nutrients, especially protein and vitamins? And does eating more low-carb foods make a difference?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 2,
          "text": "For nutrient-rich breast milk, mothers should consume at least 25 extra grams of protein daily from sources like lean meats, fish, legumes, nuts, and eggs. A varied diet of fruits, vegetables, and whole grains is vital for vitamins like A, B complex (B12 is key, especially for vegans), C, and D. While some breast milk mineral levels are stable, B vitamin levels depend on maternal intake. Low-carb diets, if calorie-restrictive, can affect the mother's energy and nutrient status, so ensure adequate carbohydrates and overall macronutrients.",
