      {
        "turn": 8,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really interesting! Is it possible for someone to run out of milk when they are breastfeeding? Sometimes I wonder how supply works.",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "No, it's generally not possible to \"run out\" of breast milk. Milk production works on a \"supply and demand\" basis: the more milk is removed from the breast, the more your body produces. Breasts are never truly empty; milk is continuously made, and the emptier the breast, the faster it produces milk. Hormones like prolactin and oxytocin are key to this process. While factors like infrequent feeding or poor latch can lead to low supply, consistent feeding on demand ensures continued production.",
          "citations": {
