      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I like that tip about calcium, since I\u2019m already worried I don't get enough. Are there problems if I keep eating mostly white bread and white rice during breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : it \u2019 s not harmful to your baby or milk supply to eat mostly white bread and white rice. but they \u2019 re refined grains, so you \u2019 ll miss out on fiber and some nutrients that help your energy, digestion, and overall recovery. to support your calcium goal : - aim for about 1, 000 mg calcium / day. good picks : milk, yogurt, cheese ; calcium \u2011 fortified soy / almond / oat drinks ; calcium \u2011 set tofu ; leafy greens ; beans / lentils ; nuts / seeds ; dried fruit ; tinned salmon or sardines with bones ; brown / wholemeal bread can help too. - take 10 mcg ( 400 iu ) vitamin d daily while breastfeeding, and give your baby 10 mcg daily. vitamin d helps you absorb calcium. simple upgrades if you prefer white grains : - try a 50 / 50 loaf, or mix white and brown rice. - add oats, beans, or lentils to meals for fiber and protein. - pair white rice with protein and veg to steady energy. extras : - keep caffeine to about 200 mg / day ( \u22482 coffees ). - snack ideas with calcium / protein :",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:2": -1.9529297351837158,
            "clueweb22-en0014-81-15963:5": -1.9813472032546997,
            "clueweb22-en0016-50-05532:9": -2.2525973320007324,
            "clueweb22-en0014-81-15963:4": -2.4117777347564697,
            "clueweb22-en0040-50-01153:12": -2.470288038253784
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that's exactly what I wanted to know! Another thing: can someone actually run out of milk when they're breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: you don\u2019t \u201crun out\u201d of milk in a day-to-day sense. Breasts make milk continuously; supply rises to match how often and how well milk is removed.  Normal things that don\u2019t mean low supply: - Cluster feeding (especially evenings, growth spurts) - Softer breasts or less leaking after a few weeks - Getting little with a pump (babies remove milk better than pumps)  How to tell baby is getting enough: - 6\u20138 wet nappies in 24 hours with some dirty ones - 8\u201312 feeds per 24 hours, waking and feeding vigorously - Back to birth weight by about 2 weeks; then gaining ~150 g/week in the first 3 months - Settles after most feeds  If supply seems low (it\u2019s usually temporary): - Feed more often and on cue; ensure a deep latch - Offer both breasts; start the next feed on the last side - Skin-to-skin; avoid long gaps between feeds - Consider pumping after feeds and get lactation support  Seek help if diapers are few, weight gain is poor, baby is very sleepy or dehydrated.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0044-22-08373:2": -3.6959309577941895,
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:2": -3.861382246017456,
