  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Is breastfeeding bad for infants in any way?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Breastfeeding is overall very beneficial for infants and mothers, but there are a few situations in which it can pose risks.  Main potential harms - Transfer of drugs or substances from mother to milk: some prescription medicines, illicit drugs (e.g., cocaine), and certain treatments can pass into breast milk and harm an infant whose metabolism is immature. Topical application of substances to the nipple can be especially dangerous. - Specific drug risks: some medications can cause serious problems in infants with enzyme deficiencies (for example, causing hemolysis in G6PD\u2011deficient babies); in those cases breastfeeding may be contraindicated until risk has passed. - Infectious risks: certain infections in the mother can be transmitted via breast milk (needs case\u2011by\u2011case evaluation).  What to do - Discuss all medications, supplements, smoking/alcohol and any drug use with your clinician or a lactation specialist. - If you or your baby have special concerns (e.g., known G6PD deficiency, current illicit drug use, or maternal infection), get individualized medical advice.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0040-95-10127:1": 30.98390007019043,
            "clueweb22-en0008-39-05957:5": 30.197999954223633,
            "clueweb22-en0003-41-06988:8": 29.538799285888672,
            "clueweb22-en0008-39-05957:4": 29.435400009155273,
            "clueweb22-en0040-95-10127:0": 29.07939910888672
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that makes sense. Speaking of diet, do I need to change what I eat during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 you should aim to adjust your eating in pregnancy to meet extra nutrient needs and reduce some risks. Key points:  - Eat a balanced, nutrient-dense diet: plenty of whole grains, lean protein, fruits, vegetables, dairy or calcium-rich alternatives, and healthy fats.   - Take a prenatal vitamin with folic acid (to prevent neural-tube defects) and discuss iron and vitamin D with your clinician.   - Increase calories modestly (roughly +300 kcal/day in 2nd\u20133rd trimester) and eat more protein.   - Focus on key nutrients: folate, iron, calcium, vitamin D, DHA (omega\u20113), and B12 if vegetarian/vegan.   - Avoid alcohol entirely, limit caffeine (~<200 mg/day), and avoid raw/undercooked meat, unpasteurized dairy, raw fish/shellfish, raw sprouts, and high\u2011mercury fish. Heat deli meats before eating.   - Manage nausea with small, frequent meals and plain carbs if needed.   - Talk with your provider about any supplements, medications, or special diets for personalized guidance.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0030-32-17514:12": 36.58440017700195,
            "clueweb22-en0034-66-17702:4": 36.2677001953125,
            "clueweb22-en0017-70-16413:0": 35.7327995300293,
            "clueweb22-en0008-96-17009:0": 35.66680145263672,
            "clueweb22-en0006-72-05776:4": 35.08539962768555
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "What about breastfeeding\u2014do I need to eat differently once the baby is born?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You don't need a special or restrictive diet while breastfeeding, but you should eat a varied, healthy diet and take a few simple precautions.  Practical points - Aim for a balanced diet: plenty of fruit and vegetables, whole grains, protein (meat, fish, eggs, pulses, nuts), dairy or calcium-rich alternatives, and healthy fats.   - You will need extra energy while breastfeeding, so eat nutrient-dense meals and snacks to maintain your supply and energy.   - Drink plenty of fluids\u2014keep a drink nearby when you feed.   - Take a daily vitamin D supplement (about 10 mcg) and consider continuing your prenatal vitamin if advised by your clinician.   - Limit alcohol and caffeine, avoid smoking and recreational drugs, and always check prescription or herbal medicines with your clinician before breastfeeding.   - If your baby seems unsettled after you eat a particular food, talk with your baby\u2019s doctor; a supervised elimination trial may help identify a cause without harming your nutrition.  If you have special dietary needs (vegan/vegetarian) or concerns, ask a clinician or nutritionist for tailored advice.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0033-02-07290:0": 41.33489990234375,
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:1": 40.88290023803711,
            "clueweb22-en0016-50-05532:1": 38.499000549316406,
            "clueweb22-en0033-22-04249:14": 38.46540069580078,
            "clueweb22-en0038-42-14114:0": 38.032798767089844
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 milk supply can fall, and in some cases people feel like they\u2019ve \u201crun out.\u201d Often it\u2019s temporary and fixable. Common causes: infrequent or incomplete breast emptying (including long gaps or over\u2011reliance on one feeding position), poor latch/position, stress/fatigue, illness or mastitis, certain medications, and rarely underlying medical issues (prior breast surgery, hormonal problems, insufficient glandular tissue). One breast can also make less milk than the other.  What helps: nurse or pump more frequently (empty at least every 2\u20133 hours), check and improve latch/position, try power\u2011pumping and warm compresses, manage stress/sleep/hydration, treat infections promptly, and consider short\u2011term supplementation if needed. Most supplies respond to increased stimulation. If concern persists, see an IBCLC/lactation consultant or your clinician to identify causes and make a plan.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-79-06723:2": 32.05630111694336,
            "clueweb22-en0012-79-06723:3": 31.807300567626953,
            "clueweb22-en0046-85-11827:2": 31.488000869750977,
            "clueweb22-en0046-85-11827:1": 30.466699600219727,
            "clueweb22-en0036-41-07823:1": 30.45949935913086
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you for the detailed conversation and thoughtful questions! If you have any more concerns in the future, feel free to reach out. Wishing you all the best\u2014take care and farewell!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
