  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 milk supply can fall, and in some cases people feel like they\u2019ve \u201crun out.\u201d Often it\u2019s temporary and fixable. Common causes: infrequent or incomplete breast emptying (including long gaps or over\u2011reliance on one feeding position), poor latch/position, stress/fatigue, illness or mastitis, certain medications, and rarely underlying medical issues (prior breast surgery, hormonal problems, insufficient glandular tissue). One breast can also make less milk than the other.  What helps: nurse or pump more frequently (empty at least every 2\u20133 hours), check and improve latch/position, try power\u2011pumping and warm compresses, manage stress/sleep/hydration, treat infections promptly, and consider short\u2011term supplementation if needed. Most supplies respond to increased stimulation. If concern persists, see an IBCLC/lactation consultant or your clinician to identify causes and make a plan.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0012-79-06723:2": 32.05630111694336,
            "clueweb22-en0012-79-06723:3": 31.807300567626953,
            "clueweb22-en0046-85-11827:2": 31.488000869750977,
            "clueweb22-en0046-85-11827:1": 30.466699600219727,
            "clueweb22-en0036-41-07823:1": 30.45949935913086
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      }
    ],
    "judgment": {
      "engagement": "Effective Collaborator",
      "relevance-and-usefulness": "Fully Meets",
      "overall-subtopic-quality": "Exceptional",
      "subtopic-justification": "Response gives causes for low milk supply suggest remedies. ",
