  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Is breastfeeding ever not good for infants?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 usually breastfeeding is best, but there are some situations when it\u2019s not recommended or may be unsafe. Common examples:  - Maternal HIV infection (in the U.S. breastfeeding is generally not advised because the virus can be passed in milk).  - Infants with rare metabolic disorders who cannot process components of breast milk and need special formula.  - When a mother is taking certain medications that can harm the infant; in those cases a clinician may advise formula-feeding or temporary cessation.  For other maternal infections or illnesses the risk/benefit is weighed case-by-case. Infant formula is a safe, nutritionally adequate alternative, and parents should be supported in their choice. Talk with your obstetrician, pediatrician, or a lactation consultant to get personalized advice based on the specific infection, medication or infant condition.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0028-32-19013:5": 2.543966054916382,
            "clueweb22-en0028-32-19013:4": 1.13027822971344,
            "clueweb22-en0041-35-11580:0": -0.20913171768188477,
            "clueweb22-en0007-21-05565:3": -0.43345344066619873,
            "clueweb22-en0017-23-17704:4": -0.7697842121124268
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that makes sense. Do you know if I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 you should adjust your diet in pregnancy to meet higher needs, especially for folic acid, iron, calcium/vitamin D, and protein.  Quick practical tips: - Take a daily prenatal vitamin with 0.4 mg folic acid (and during pregnancy one that supplies ~16\u201320 mg iron). Check with your clinician. - Eat a balanced mix from the five groups (fruits, veg, grains, protein foods, dairy). Aim for protein at each meal \u2014 pregnancy needs rise by about 25 g/day in the 2nd/3rd trimesters (roughly 71 g/day total). - For calcium: include milk, yogurt, cheese, fortified plant milks, tofu, and leafy greens. Aim for about three dairy-equivalent portions daily. - Boost iron absorption by pairing non-meat iron sources with vitamin C (your orange juice is helpful). Avoid tea/coffee with meals \u2014 wait 1\u20132 hours. - Food safety: avoid unpasteurized cheeses, raw/undercooked meats/fish, and other high\u2011risk foods.  Talk with your OB or a dietitian for personalized calorie/weight\u2011gain and supplement advice.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0037-32-07758:1": -3.547865629196167,
            "clueweb22-en0016-20-10648:0": -4.055778503417969,
            "clueweb22-en0016-13-12550:4": -4.14607572555542,
            "clueweb22-en0005-08-00127:5": -4.160179615020752,
            "clueweb22-en0008-09-13508:1": -4.309173583984375
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Got it! So, what about when breastfeeding\u2014do I need a specific diet, or just eat healthy in general? Are there foods to avoid?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "You don\u2019t need a special breastfeeding diet\u2014just a healthy, balanced one. Focus on: - Plenty of fruit and vegetables, protein at each meal (meat, fish, eggs, beans, tofu), wholegrains (choose wholegrain breads, brown rice, oats) and some dairy or fortified alternatives for calcium. - Calcium sources: milk, yogurt, cheese or fortified plant milks, tofu, brown bread, pulses and dried fruit. Aim for about three dairy-equivalent portions a day if you\u2019re concerned about calcium. - Vitamin D: a daily supplement of 10 mcg is recommended for breastfeeding women unless your clinician advises otherwise.  Avoid extreme calorie restriction or crash diets (don\u2019t diet hard for at least a couple of weeks after birth); too few calories can reduce energy and sometimes affect supply. Some foods/drinks and medications can pass into milk and may upset a sensitive baby (cow\u2019s\u2011milk protein is a common culprit). Check any prescription or herbal remedies, and alcohol rules, with your clinician or a lactation consultant.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:2": 2.652942657470703,
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:6": 2.4147555828094482,
            "clueweb22-en0042-76-06111:16": 2.050473690032959,
            "clueweb22-en0038-01-04875:13": 1.7013919353485107,
            "clueweb22-en0029-93-12021:1": 1.3833740949630737
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's helpful\u2014thanks! Can a mom actually run out of breast milk if she's breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: usually no \u2014 if a mother breastfeeds frequently on demand, her body will make enough milk. Milk production works by supply and demand: the more milk removed, the more is made.  When low supply does occur it\u2019s often from things that reduce milk removal or interrupt feeding: long gaps between feeds, early or unnecessary bottle supplements or pacifier use, poor latch/inefficient sucking, pumping then not removing milk for long periods, maternal illness, heavy postpartum bleeding/retained placenta, past breast surgery, or (rarely) insufficient glandular tissue. Sometimes mothers simply perceive low supply because babies cluster\u2011feed or sleep differently \u2014 supplementing with formula can then cause a real drop in supply.  What to do: nurse frequently (about 8\u201312 feeds/day), ensure a good latch, avoid unnecessary supplementation, hand\u2011express or pump if separated from baby, and get help from a lactation consultant or your pediatrician if you\u2019re worried. Most supply problems are temporary and treatable.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0018-87-03256:16": 1.9005661010742188,
            "clueweb22-en0037-76-04586:3": 1.0269657373428345,
            "clueweb22-en0042-04-04543:2": -1.010082483291626,
            "clueweb22-en0037-76-04586:2": -1.1896847486495972,
            "clueweb22-en0021-55-01275:2": -1.2840375900268555
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you for all your thoughtful questions and detailed answers! If you have any more, feel free to ask. Wishing you all the best\u2014farewell!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
