  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5mini-no-no-gpt5mini",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "5-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I have a question about breastfeeding. Can it be harmful for a baby in any way?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is breastfeeding bad for the baby?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Breastfeeding is usually the healthiest choice for most babies, but there are a few situations where it can be harmful or needs caution:  - Certain maternal infections (HIV, untreated active TB, HTLV) can be transmitted through breastmilk.   - Some maternal medications, illicit drugs, heavy alcohol use, or high nicotine exposure can harm a baby via milk. Check any medicine with your doctor or LactMed.   - Infants with classic galactosemia must not have breastmilk.   - If a baby shows allergic reactions (often to cow\u2019s milk protein passed through milk), breastfeeding may need adjustment.   - Very low milk supply can lead to poor weight gain\u2014monitor growth and seek help if needed.   - Improperly stored/contaminated expressed milk or unpasteurized donor milk carries risk.  If you\u2019re concerned, talk to your pediatrician and a lactation consultant. They can assess risks, review medications/supplements, and help safely continue or modify feeding.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful. Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy if I already try to eat healthy?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet during pregnancy?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "if you already eat healthily, that \u2019 s a great start \u2014 pregnancy mostly needs some targeted additions and precautions. key recommendations - take a prenatal vitamin ( folic acid 400 \u2013 800 mcg early, plus iron and other micronutrients ) and review with your clinician. - increase calories modestly ( about + 300 kcal / day in 2nd \u2013 3rd trimester ) and add extra protein ( about + 20 \u2013 25 g / day ). - prioritize iron ( 27 mg / day during pregnancy ), calcium ( ~ 1000 mg / day ) and vitamin d ( 600 iu or as advised ). include nutrient - dense foods : lean protein, dairy or fortified alternatives, legumes, whole grains, fruits and vegetables. - limit caffeine ( < 200 mg / day ) and avoid alcohol, raw / undercooked meat / fish, unpasteurized dairy, high - mercury fish, and deli meats unless reheated. - if you \u2019 re interested in low - carb eating, discuss it with your provider to ensure sufficient calories, fiber and micronutrients. - avoid handling cat litter ( toxoplasmosis risk ). tell me what you",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense! Does my diet need to change again after the baby is born if I decide to breastfeed?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do I need to change my diet when breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 breastfeeding does change needs a bit, but mostly it\u2019s focused on extra energy, protein, fluids, and key nutrients.  Practical targets - Calories: about +450\u2013500 kcal/day (first 6 months) \u2014 don\u2019t crash-diet.   - Protein: +15\u201325 g/day.   - Fluids: drink to thirst; keep a water bottle nearby when nursing.   - Continue a prenatal or postnatal multivitamin (folate, B12, iron if needed).   - Calcium ~1,000 mg/day and vitamin D (600\u2013800 IU or as advised) \u2014 you mentioned concern about calcium, so include dairy/fortified alternatives, leafy greens, or supplements if needed.  Precautions - Check medicines, supplements, and herbal remedies with your clinician or LactMed.   - Limit alcohol (avoid around feeds; time pumping if you drink) and moderate caffeine.   - Watch baby for signs of milk-related allergy (blood, eczema, vomiting); discuss with pediatrician if concerned.  If you want, tell me your usual postnatal diet and I\u2019ll suggest practical swaps.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Good advice, thanks! Is it possible for someone to run out of milk while breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 many people experience a drop or loss of milk supply, though complete \"running out\" is uncommon if breastfeeding or pumping regularly. Milk production is supply-and-demand: less frequent or ineffective milk removal (poor latch, long gaps, early supplementation) usually lowers supply. Other causes include retained placenta or hormonal issues (thyroid, PCOS), certain medications or hormonal contraceptives with estrogen, breast surgery, illness, stress, significant weight loss, or dehydration.  Signs the baby isn\u2019t getting enough: poor weight gain, few wet/dirty diapers, lethargy. If supply falls, remedies include frequent nursing (on demand), ensuring a good latch, pumping after feeds, skin-to-skin, correcting tongue-tie if present, adequate calories/fluid/rest, and consulting a lactation consultant. If concerns persist, see your pediatrician and your provider to check for medical causes and safe treatment options.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 5,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thank you so much for all your helpful information and questions! If you have any more concerns in the future, feel free to ask. Wishing you and your baby all the best\u2014take care and goodbye!",
          "user_rubrik": null,
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": null,
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
