          "user_utterance": "Good advice, thanks! Is it possible for someone to run out of milk while breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can someone run out of milk when they are breastfeeding?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Yes \u2014 many people experience a drop or loss of milk supply, though complete \"running out\" is uncommon if breastfeeding or pumping regularly. Milk production is supply-and-demand: less frequent or ineffective milk removal (poor latch, long gaps, early supplementation) usually lowers supply. Other causes include retained placenta or hormonal issues (thyroid, PCOS), certain medications or hormonal contraceptives with estrogen, breast surgery, illness, stress, significant weight loss, or dehydration.  Signs the baby isn\u2019t getting enough: poor weight gain, few wet/dirty diapers, lethargy. If supply falls, remedies include frequent nursing (on demand), ensuring a good latch, pumping after feeds, skin-to-skin, correcting tongue-tie if present, adequate calories/fluid/rest, and consulting a lactation consultant. If concerns persist, see your pediatrician and your provider to check for medical causes and safe treatment options.",
